  {
   "id": "ZzM_8inV8FoJ",
   "from": "Matt Hoffman",
   "author": "1004dd6f66bb10c3",
   "date": "2012-09-06T17:42:21Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Error when fitting multivariate probit",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hi Will,\r<br>\r<br>I believe the error message here is accurate—the way you&#39;ve written\r<br>it, p(Z|...) is discontinuous, due to the step function. This causes\r<br>problems for NUTS, which really wants a continuous (and ideally\r<br>continuously differentiable) log-posterior.\r<br>\r<br>An alternative approach is to declare the latent Z variables to be\r<br>constrained to be consistent with the observed data, like so:\r<br>\r<br>parameters {\r<br>  real&lt;lower=0&gt; Zarray[n, J];\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "  real B[J, K];\r<br>  cov_matrix[J] S;\r<br>}\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "transformed parameters {\r<br>  # This is a workaround to deal with the fact\r<br>  # that we don&#39;t yet support bounded vectors\r<br>  vector[J] Z[n];\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "  for (i in 1:n)\r<br>    for (j in 1:J)\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "      Z[n, j] &lt;- Zarray[n, J];\r<br>}\r<br>model {\r<br>  ...\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "  for (i in 1:n) {\r<br>    for (j in 1:J) {\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "      Mu[i, j] &lt;- (2*Y[i,j]-1) * dot_product(B[j], X[i]);\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "    }\r<br>  }\r<br>\r<br>  S ~ inv_wishart(df, ID);\r<br>\r<br>  for (i in 1:n) {\r<br>    Z[i] ~ multi_normal(col(Mu&#39;, i), S);\r<br>  }\r<br>}\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "It&#39;s a little roundabout, but (unless I messed up somewhere) this\r<br>model is equivalent to the one you wrote.\r<br>\r<br>I&#39;ve actually had some trouble getting multivariate probit models to\r<br>converge in a reasonable amount of time in Stan, so I&#39;d be very\r<br>interested to hear if you have better luck than I did (using a\r<br>slightly different approach).\r<br>\r<br>Best,\r<br>Matt\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>On Thu, Sep 6, 2012 at 9:49 AM, William Morris &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">wkm...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hi all,\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Am trying to adapt a multivariate probit model implemented in JAGS\r<br>&gt; (attached). My attempt at an Rstan version results in the following error\r<br>&gt; message.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; TRANSLATING MODEL &#39;anon_model&#39; FROM Stan CODE TO C++ CODE NOW.\r<br>&gt; COMPILING THE C++ CODE FOR MODEL &#39;anon_model&#39; NOW.\r<br>&gt; SAMPLING FOR MODEL &#39;anon_model&#39; NOW (CHAIN 1).\r<br>&gt; Error in sampler$call_sampler(args_list[[i]]) :\r<br>&gt;     No acceptably small step size could be found. Perhaps the posterior is\r<br>&gt; not continuous?&#39;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Anything obvious I am doing wrong in the code below?\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; model &lt;- &quot;\r<br>&gt;   data {\r<br>&gt;     int n;\r<br>&gt;     int J;\r<br>&gt;     int K;\r<br>&gt;     int Y[n, J];\r<br>&gt;     real X[n, K];\r<br>&gt;     matrix[J, J] ID;\r<br>&gt;     real df;\r<br>&gt;   }\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;   parameters {\r<br>&gt;     vector[J] Z[n];\r<br>&gt;     real B[J, K];\r<br>&gt;     cov_matrix[J] S;\r<br>&gt;   }\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;   model {\r<br>&gt;     matrix[n, J] Mu;\r<br>&gt;     real p[n, J];\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     for (j in 1:J) {\r<br>&gt;       B[j] ~ normal(0, 100);\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     for (i in 1:n) {\r<br>&gt;       for (j in 1:J) {\r<br>&gt;         Mu[i, j] &lt;- dot_product(B[j], X[i]);\r<br>&gt;       }\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     S ~ inv_wishart(df, ID);\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     for (i in 1:n) {\r<br>&gt;       Z[i] ~ multi_normal(col(Mu&#39;, i), S);\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     for (i in 1:n) {\r<br>&gt;       for (j in 1:J) {\r<br>&gt;         p[i, j] &lt;- step(Z[i][j]);\r<br>&gt;       }\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     for (i in 1:n) {\r<br>&gt;       for (j in 1:J) {\r<br>&gt;         Y[i, j] ~ bernoulli(p[i, j]);\r<br>&gt;