  {
   "id": "Lc7xSY5nfdUJ",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2012-09-06T17:53:52Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Error when fitting multivariate probit",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>\r<br>On 9/6/12 12:49 PM, William Morris wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hi all,\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Am trying to adapt a multivariate probit model implemented in JAGS (attached). My attempt at an Rstan version results in\r<br>&gt; the following error message.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; TRANSLATING MODEL &#39;anon_model&#39; FROM Stan CODE TO C++ CODE NOW.\r<br>&gt; COMPILING THE C++ CODE FOR MODEL &#39;anon_model&#39; NOW.\r<br>&gt; SAMPLING FOR MODEL &#39;anon_model&#39; NOW (CHAIN 1).\r<br>&gt; Error in sampler$call_sampler(args_list[[i]]) :\r<br>&gt;      No acceptably small step size could be found. Perhaps the posterior is not continuous?&#39;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Anything obvious I am doing wrong in the code below?\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "I think your problem is in step().  Some other\r<br>code improvements suggested inline.\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; model &lt;- &quot;\r<br>&gt;    data {\r<br>&gt;      int n;\r<br>&gt;      int J;\r<br>&gt;      int K;\r<br>&gt;      int Y[n, J];\r<br>&gt;      real X[n, K];\r<br>&gt;      matrix[J, J] ID;\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "We really need an identity matrix constant.\r<br>So this could be:\r<br>\r<br>transformed data {\r<br>    matrix[J,J] ID;\r<br>    ID &lt;- unit_matrix(J);\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "}\r<br>\r<br>&gt;      real df;\r<br>&gt;    }\r<br>&gt;    parameters {\r<br>&gt;      vector[J] Z[n];\r<br>&gt;      real B[J, K];\r<br>&gt;      cov_matrix[J] S;\r<br>&gt;    }\r<br>&gt;    model {\r<br>&gt;      matrix[n, J] Mu;\r<br>&gt;      real p[n, J];\r<br>&gt;      for (j in 1:J) {\r<br>&gt;        B[j] ~ normal(0, 100);\r<br>&gt;      }\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "This can be vectorized to B ~ normal(0,100);\r<br>\r<br>You might want a slightly tighter prior on B.\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt;      for (i in 1:n) {\r<br>&gt;        for (j in 1:J) {\r<br>&gt;          Mu[i, j] &lt;- dot_product(B[j], X[i]);\r<br>&gt;        }\r<br>&gt;      }\r<br>&gt;      S ~ inv_wishart(df, ID);\r<br>&gt;      for (i in 1:n) {\r<br>&gt;        Z[i] ~ multi_normal(col(Mu&#39;, i), S);\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Doing Mu[i]&#39;, equivalently row(Mu,i)&#39;,\r<br>is more efficient than col(Mu&#39;,i).  The issue is\r<br>that Mu&#39; makes a copy of the whole matrix during\r<br>the transpose.\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt;      }\r<br>&gt;      for (i in 1:n) {\r<br>&gt;        for (j in 1:J) {\r<br>&gt;          p[i, j] &lt;- step(Z[i][j]);\r<br>&gt;        }\r<br>&gt;      }\r<br>&gt;      for (i in 1:n) {\r<br>&gt;        for (j in 1:J) {\r<br>&gt;          Y[i, j] ~ bernoulli(p[i, j]);\r<br>&gt;        }\r<br>&gt;      }\r<br>&gt;    }\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Unless you need to see the p[i,j], I&#39;d suggest removing\r<br>the first loop and replacing the inner part of the second\r<br>with bernoulli(step(Z[i,j]));\r<br>\r<br>But now that I see what&#39;s up here, I think the step function\r<br>is probably your problem.  It returns either 0 or 1.\r<br>\r<br>I think what you want to do is more like:\r<br>\r<br>    Y[i,j] ~ bernoulli(inv_logit(Z[i,j]));\r<br>\r<br>Or you can use Phi instead of inv_logit if you prefer\r<br>probit.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "Uu5Zaml_Wy0J",
   "from": "Matt Hoffman",
   "author": "1004dd6f66bb10c3",
   "date": "2012-09-06T17:59:49Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Error when fitting multivariate probit",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "I think the step=&gt;bernoulli thing was just a way of implementing the\r<br>multivariate probit model. Using inv_logit() instead would result in a\r<br>sort of overdispersed probit model, which I don&#39;t think is what was\r<br>desired. I just sent an email summarizing our earlier discussion from\r<br>stan-dev about my issues with MV probits.\r<br>\r<br>Matt\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>On Thu, Sep 6, 2012 at 10:53 AM, Bob Carpenter &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ca...@alias-i.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; On 9/6/12 12:49 PM, William Morris wrote:\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; Hi all,\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; Am trying to adapt a multivariate probit model implemented in JAGS\r<br>&gt;&gt; (attached). My attempt at an Rstan version results in\r<br>&gt;&gt; the following error message.\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; TRANSLATING MODEL &#39;anon_model&#39; FROM Stan CODE TO C++ CODE NOW.\r<br>&gt;&gt; COMPILING THE C++ CODE FOR MODEL &#39;anon_model&#39; NOW.\r<br>&gt;&gt; SAMPLING FOR MODEL &#39;anon_model&#39; NOW (CHAIN 1).\r<br>&gt;&gt; Error in sampler$call_sampler(args_list[[i]]) :\r<br>&gt;&gt;      No acceptably small step size could be found. Perhaps the posterior\r<br>&gt;&gt; is not continuous?&#39;\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; Anything obvious I am doing wrong in the code below?\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; I think your problem is in step().  Some other\r<br>&gt; code improvements suggested inline.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; model &lt;- &quot;\r<br>&gt;&gt;    data {\r<br>&gt;&gt;      int n;\r<br>&gt;&gt;      int J;\r<br>&gt;&gt;      int K;\r<br>&gt;&gt;      int Y[n, J];\r<br>&gt;&gt;      real X[n, K];\r<br>&gt;&gt;      matrix[J, J] ID;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; We really need an identity matrix constant.\r<br>&gt; So this could be:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; transformed data {\r<br>&gt;    matrix[J,J] ID;\r<br>&gt;    ID &lt;- unit_matrix(J);\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt;      real df;\r<br>&gt;&gt;    }\r<br>&gt;&gt;    parameters {\r<br>&gt;&gt;      vector[J] Z[n];\r<br>&gt;&gt;      real B[J, K];\r<br>&gt;&gt;      cov_matrix[J] S;\r<br>&gt;&gt;    }\r<br>&gt;&gt;    model {\r<br>&gt;&gt;      matrix[n, J] Mu;\r<br>&gt;&gt;      real p[n, J];\r<br>&gt;&gt;      for (j in 1:J) {\r<br>&gt;&gt;        B[j] ~ normal(0, 100);\r<br>&gt;&gt;      }\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; This can be vectorized to B ~ normal(0,100);\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; You might want a slightly tighter prior on B.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt;      for (i in 1:n) {\r<br>&gt;&gt;        for (j in 1:J) {\r<br>&gt;&gt;          Mu[i, j] &lt;- dot_product(B[j], X[i]);\r<br>&gt;&gt;        }\r<br>&gt;&gt;      }\r<br>&gt;&gt;      S ~ inv_wishart(df, ID);\r<br>&gt;&gt;      for (i in 1:n) {\r<br>&gt;&gt;        Z[i] ~ multi_normal(col(Mu&#39;, i), S);\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Doing Mu[i]&#39;, equivalently row(Mu,i)&#39;,\r<br>&gt; is more efficient than col(Mu&#39;,i).  The issue is\r<br>&gt; that Mu&#39; makes a copy of the whole matrix during\r<br>&gt; the transpose.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt;      }\r<br>&gt;&gt;      for (i in 1:n) {\r<br>&gt;&gt;        for (j in 1:J) {\r<br>&gt;&gt;          p[i, j] &lt;- step(Z[i][j]);\r<br>&gt;&gt;        }\r<br>&gt;&gt;      }\r<br>&gt;&gt;      for (i in 1:n) {\r<br>&gt;&gt;        for (j in 1:J) {\r<br>&gt;&gt;          Y[i, j] ~ bernoulli(p[i, j]);\r<br>&gt;&gt;        }\r<br>&gt;&gt;      }\r<br>&gt;&gt;    }\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Unless you need to see the p[i,j], I&#39;d suggest removing\r<br>&gt; the first loop and replacing the inner part of the second\r<br>&gt; with bernoulli(step(Z[i,j]));\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; But now that I see what&#39;s up here, I think the step function\r<br>&gt; is probably your problem.  It returns either 0 or 1.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; I think what you want to do is more like:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;    Y[i,j] ~ bernoulli(inv_logit(Z[i,j]));\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Or you can use Phi instead of inv_logit if you prefer\r<br>&gt; probit.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; - Bob\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
